\details{
Behind the scenes, a pair of vector images (outline & fill) are
converted into Cairo graphics library SVG files, then into grid
graphical objects (grobs) for use in a ggplot2 layer.

By default, the "violin" shape is used.

If the shape is mapped to a variable, e.g. \code{aes(shape = factor(cyl))}, then
\code{scale_shape_manual()} is also required to explicitly name the desired
shapes as a character vector (see examples). This is because standard
shapes are associated with a number, e.g. a circle is 19, whereas
\code{geom_casting()} shapes are associated only with character strings.

Aesthetics that vary per observation, e.g. a continuous \code{fill}, are
respected for each individual shape, whether grouping is implicit or
explicitly shared with \code{group = 1}. A \code{fill} of \code{"transparent"} renders
the outline only: for shapes such as the "bowl" set, this hides the steam
while retaining the bowl outline.

In addition to the supported aesthetics below, \code{nudge_x}, \code{nudge_y},
\code{hjust} and \code{vjust} are also respected.
}
